Spencer raised an interesting question (to me, at least):  Am I allow to
take an S function that is in the standard S-PLUS distribution and modify it
for my own use?  I believe quite a number of people are used to customizing
built-in S functions to suit their own purpose.  For example, I recall that
when I was doing some simulation back in grad school, I had to modify
sample() so that if it is given a single number, say 10, it does not
interpret that as sampling from 1:10, but just return 10 instead.  (Of
course, I would call this mysample() to avoid conflict with the system
verion.)  Does this constitute violation of Insightful's copyright/IP/etc.?

Best,
Andy

> From: Spencer Graves
> 
>       1.  I think I see now the source of your problem:  In 
> [~w*exp(-k1*t)+(1-w)*exp(-k2*t)], if w=0, there is no 
> information in the 
> data to estimate k1.  In that case, nls may find a singularity and 
> refuse to return an answer.  (Similarly, if w=1, there is no 
> information 
> to estimate k2.)  In theory, this singularity can be broken by 
> specifying an informative prior on k1 and k2.  However, I 
> don't know how 
